<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Atatürk's reforms</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Kemalist reforms</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Atatürk's reforms were the political, legal, cultural and social changes carried out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> between 1923 and 1938 to build a secular, national and modern state in place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. They included the abolition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in 1924, the closure of religious courts and orders, a civil code based on Swiss law, the adoption of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabet in 1928 and the vote for women in national elections in 1934. Their guiding principles, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemalism">Kemalism</a>, were written into the constitution in 1937.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The reforms followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkish-war-of-independence">Turkish War of Independence</a> of 1919 to 1922, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal Atatürk</a> united much of the Turkish Muslim population and expelled the foreign forces occupying what the Turkish National Movement considered the Turkish homeland. In 1923 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and recognised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a>. The Ottoman monarchy had been abolished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> government, and on 1 November 1922 the sultanate was abolished.</p><p>The reformers chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a> as the new capital in 1923, rejecting what they saw as the corruption of cosmopolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a> and choosing a more central site. Historians such as Erik-Jan Zürcher and Şerif Mardin stress continuities with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> period, which began in 1839, with the schooling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdul-hamid-ii">Abdul Hamid II</a> and with the Young Turks, rather than treating 1923 as the start of modernisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The republic was proclaimed on 29 October 1923, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> was abolished on 3 March 1924, the same day education was centralised. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">Sharia</a> courts were abolished on 8 April 1924. The new Constitution of 1924 replaced that of 1921, and by 1928 the constitutional reference to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the state religion had been removed. All medreses were placed under the Ministry of National Education.</p><p>Men's headgear was changed on 25 November 1925, and ashar, a tax in kind, was abolished on 17 February 1925. The Turkish Historical Society was founded in 1931 and the Turkish Language Association on 12 July 1932, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> script was adopted, together with a purging of foreign loan words.</p><p>The Surname Law of 21 June 1934 required all citizens to adopt fixed hereditary surnames, and on 26 November 1934 titles and by-names were abolished. On 5 December 1934 women received full political rights to vote and be elected. State-owned factories and banks were founded, such as Türkiye İş Bankası in 1924, and the First Five Year Development Plan began on 1 December 1933. The literacy rate rose from 9% to 33% in ten years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-peoples-party">Republican People's Party</a> ruled as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> from 1923 to 1945. After Atatürk's death in 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> took over and extended the reforms, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a> won the elections of 1950. Historians disagree about the reforms. Patrick Kinross held that most of the population accepted them, while Mustafa Tuna argued that they did not reach rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, where more than 80% of the population lived.</p><p>Critics point to the one-party regime, to the banning of the Progressive Republican Party in June 1925 after the Sheikh Said rebellion, and to the assimilation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> that Uğur Ümit Üngör describes. The ideology that came to be known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kemalism">Kemalism</a> stayed central to the political system, and the military has seen itself as its guardian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Atatürk's reforms” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Atatürk's reforms</b> met the Sheikh Said rebellion of 1925 and the Dersim rebellion of 1937 among Kurdish and Zaza tribes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Ottoman Empire following World War I and the founding of the Republic of Turkey, widespread language reforms instituted by Mustafa Kemal Atatürk, part of the larger framework of <b>Atatürk’s reforms</b>, replaced many Persian and Arabic loanwords with Turkish equivalents and the Perso-Arabic script with an extended Latin alphabet.</blockquote><a href="/language/ottoman-turkish">Ottoman Turkish · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
